<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aksaray</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aksaray is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, at the foot of the volcano Hasan Dağı, and capital of the province of the same name. Known in Roman times as Archelais, it flourished under the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, which built great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a> such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Han on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>. Nearby lie the Ihlara Valley, with its rock-cut churches, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> mound of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acemhoyuk">Acemhöyük</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aksaray.jpg" alt="Aksaray" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NuriCanY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AksarayClockTower.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Aksaray stands in the Central Anatolia region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, at an average elevation of 980 metres, with the Melendiz river running through it. Mount Hasan rises to 3,268 metres. The city was an important stopover on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> across Anatolia for centuries. Its name means white palace in Turkish. In antiquity the area was called Archelais Garsaura, which became Taksara under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and then Aksaray.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ancient Hittite texts first mention the place as Šinaḫuttum or Šinuhtu. The Aksaray Stela, written in Luwian hieroglyphs, was dedicated by a king named Kiyakiyas, who described his prosperity and the benevolence of the weather god Tarhunzas. He may be the king Kiyakki of Šinuḫtu who ruled before 718 BCE, when the Neo-Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> took the city and deported him.</p><p>The Hittite name Kurşaura became Garsaura, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> mentions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archelaus-of-cappadocia">Archelaus of Cappadocia</a>, the last Cappadocian king, renamed the town Archelaïs, and in Roman times it was a colony and a bishopric. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> attended councils from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> in 325 to the Trullan Council of 692.</p><p>In Byzantine times, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebinkarahisar">Koloneia</a>, it was an important military centre. Between 1081 and 1084 it formed part of the domain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish">Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</a>, and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, whose sultans left important landmarks in and around the town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who visited in the 14th century, called it a beautiful city surrounded by waterways and gardens, with water brought to the houses, and noted its class of Muslim traders.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> raided it in 1277 and killed or enslaved 6,000 of its people. İshak Pasha incorporated it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1470 after a long struggle against the Karamanids, and many inhabitants were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where a district also came to be named Aksaray. The nearby Lake Tuz, the main source of salt for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, helped the town to prosper.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Aksaray is the seat of Aksaray Province, with 247,147 inhabitants in 2021. The Red Minaret Mosque has a Seljuk minaret of 1236 from the reign of Aläettin Keykubat, and the Zincirye Medresesi, a Koranic school built by the bey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a> in 1345, now serves as the local library. The Aksaray Grand Mosque dates from 1408-09 and the Karamanoğlu dynasty, and the Hakiki Yusuf Baba complex holds the tombs of the 14th-century Somuncu Baba and the scholar Cemaleddin'i Aksaray.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aksaray” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Towns such as Konya, Kayseri, Sivas, <b>Aksaray</b> and Malatya grew as centres of crafts and commerce, and the guild-like brotherhoods of the akhis organised urban artisans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Burushattum appears in Old Assyrian trade letters of the 20th and 19th centuries BCE as a wealthy city in south-central Anatolia, probably at Acemhöyük near <b>Aksaray</b>, though the identification is debated.</blockquote><a href="/state/purushanda">Purushanda</a></li></ul></section>
